I": "10.7560/720466", "type": "edited-book", "created": {"date-parts": [[2020, 12, 26]], "date-time": "2020-12-26T18:21:35Z", "timestamp": 1609006895000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 2617, "title": ["Speech Genres and Other Late Essays"], "author": [{"given": "M. M.", "family": "Bakhtin", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "issued": {"date-parts": [[1986, 12, 31]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[1986, 12, 31]]}}, {"abstract": "<jats:p>Nearly perfect speech recognition was observed under conditions of greatly reduced spectral information. Temporal envelopes of speech were extracted from broad frequency bands and were used to modulate noises of the same bandwidths. This manipulation preserved temporal envelope cues in each band but restricted the listener to severely degraded information on the distribution of spectral energy. The identification of consonants, vowels, and words in simple sentences improved markedly as the number of bands increased; high speech recognition performance was obtained with only three bands of modulated noise. Thus, the presentation of a dynamic temporal pattern in only a few broad spectral regions is sufficient for the recognition of speech.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1126/science.270.5234.303", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2006, 10, 27]], "date-time": "2006-10-27T18:19:42Z", "timestamp": 1161973182000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 2570, "title": ["Speech Recognition with Primarily Temporal Cues"], "author": [{"given": "Robert V.", "family": "Shannon", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "House Ear Institute, 2100 West Third Street, Los Angeles, CA 90057, USA."}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Fan-Gang", "family": "Zeng", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "House Ear Institute, 2100 West Third Street, Los Angeles, CA 90057, USA."}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Vivek", "family": "Kamath", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "House Ear Institute, 2100 West Third Street, Los Angeles, CA 90057, USA."}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "John", "family": "Wygonski", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "House Ear Institute, 2100 West Third Street, Los Angeles, CA 90057, USA."}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Michael", "family": "Ekelid", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "House Ear Institute, 2100 West Third Street, Los Angeles, CA 90057, USA."}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["Science"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[1995, 10, 13]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[1995, 10, 13]]}}, {"DOI": "10.1037/h0020279", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2006, 6, 7]], "date-time": "2006-06-07T19:25:21Z", "timestamp": 1149708321000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 2499, "title": ["Perception of the speech code."], "author": [{"given": "A. M.", "family": "Liberman", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "F. S.", "family": "Cooper", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "D. P.", "family": "Shankweiler", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "M.", "family": "Studdert-Kennedy", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["Psychological Review"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[1967]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[1967]]}}, {"abstract": "<jats:p>Allport (1954) recognized that attachment to one's ingroups does not necessarily require hostility toward outgroups. Yet the prevailing approach to the study of ethnocentrism, ingroup bias, and prejudice presumes that ingroup love and outgroup hate are reciprocally related. Findings from both cross‐cultural research and laboratory experiments support the alternative view that ingroup identification is independent of negative attitudes toward outgroups and that much ingroup bias and intergroup discrimination is motivated by preferential treatment of ingroup members rather than direct hostility toward outgroup members. Thus to understand the roots of prejudice and discrimination requires first of all a better understanding of the functions that ingroup formation and identification serve for human beings. This article reviews research and theory on the motivations for maintenance of ingroup boundaries and the implications of ingroup boundary protection for intergroup relations, conflict, and conflict prevention.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1111/0022-4537.00126", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2003, 3, 12]], "date-time": "2003-03-12T03:08:09Z", "timestamp": 1047438489000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 2488, "title": ["The Psychology of Prejudice: Ingroup Love and Outgroup Hate?"], "author": [{"given": "Marilynn B.", "family": "Brewer", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["Journal of Social Issues"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[1999, 1]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[1999, 1]]}}, {"DOI": "10.21437/interspeech.2020-3015", "type": "proceedings-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2020, 10, 27]], "date-time": "2020-10-27T05:22:11Z", "timestamp": 1603776131000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 2468, "title": ["Conformer: Convolution-augmented Transformer for Speech Recognition"], "author": [{"given": "Anmol", "family": "Gulati", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "James", "family": "Qin", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Chung-Cheng", "family": "Chiu", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Niki", "family": "Parmar", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Yu", "family": "Zhang", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Jiahui", "family": "Yu", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Wei", "family": "Han", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Shibo", "family": "Wang", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Zhengdong", "family": "Zhang", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Yonghui", "family": "Wu", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Ruoming", "family": "Pang", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["Interspeech 2020"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2020, 10, 25]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[2020, 10, 25]]}}, {"DOI": "10.21437/interspeech.2019-2680", "type": "proceedings-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2019, 9, 13]], "date-time": "2019-09-13T16:32:51Z", "timestamp": 1568392371000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 2400, "title": ["SpecAugment: A Simple Data Augmentation Method for Automatic Speech Recognition"], "author": [{"given": "Daniel S.", "family": "Park", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "William", "family": "Chan", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Yu", "family": "Zhang", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Chung-Cheng", "family": "Chiu", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Barret", "family": "Zoph", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Ekin D.", "family": "Cubuk", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Quoc V.", "family": "Le", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["Interspeech 2019"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2019, 9, 15]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[2019, 9, 15]]}}, {"abstract": "<jats:p>\n                    The mitochondrion is at the core of cellular energy metabolism, being the site of most ATP generation. Calcium is a key regulator of mitochondrial function and acts at several levels within the organelle to stimulate ATP synthesis. However, the dysregulation of mitochondrial Ca\n                    <jats:sup>2+</jats:sup>\n                    homeostasis is now recognized to play a key role in several pathologies. For example, mitochondrial matrix Ca\n                    <jats:sup>2+</jats:sup>\n                    overload can lead to enhanced generation of reactive oxygen species, triggering of the permeability transition pore, and cytochrome c release, leading to apoptosis. Despite progress regarding the independent roles of both Ca\n                    <jats:sup>2+</jats:sup>\n                    and mitochondrial dysfunction in disease, the molecular mechanisms by which Ca\n                    <jats:sup>2+</jats:sup>\n                    can elicit mitochondrial dysfunction remain elusive. This review highlights the delicate balance between the positive and negative effects of Ca\n                    <jats:sup>2+</jats:sup>\n                    and the signaling events that perturb this balance. Overall, a “two-hit” hypothesis is developed, in which Ca\n                    <jats:sup>2+</jats:sup>\n                    plus another pathological stimulus can bring about mitochondrial dysfunction.\n                  </jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1152/ajpcell.00139.2004", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2004, 9, 8]], "date-time": "2004-09-08T13:22:58Z", "timestamp": 1094649778000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 2287, "title": ["Calcium, ATP, and ROS: a mitochondrial love-hate triangle"], "author": [{"given": "Paul S.", "family": "Brookes", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Yisang", "family": "Yoon", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "James L.", "family": "Robotham", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "M. W.", "family": "Anders", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Shey-Shing", "family": "Sheu", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["American Journal of Physiology-Cell Physiology"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2004, 10]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[2004, 10]]}}, {"DOI": "10.1109/icassp.2001.941023", "title": ["Perceptual evaluation of speech quality (PESQ)-a new method for speech quality assessment of telephone networks and codecs"], "container-title": ["2001 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech, and Signal Processing. Proceedings (Cat. No.01CH37221)"], "author": [{"given": "A.W.", "family": "Rix", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "J.G.", "family": "Beerends", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "M.P.", "family": "Hollier", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "A.P.", "family": "Hekstra", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "issued": {"date-parts": [[null]]}, "created": {"date-parts": [[2002, 11, 13]], "date-time": "2002-11-13T12:02:15Z", "timestamp": 1037188935000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 2208, "type": "proceedings-article"}, {"DOI": "10.1109/tasl.2011.2134090", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2011, 4, 6]], "date-time": "2011-04-06T18:14:43Z", "timestamp": 1302113683000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 2176, "title": ["Context-Dependent Pre-Trained Deep Neural Networks for Large-Vocabulary Speech Recognition"], "author": [{"given": "G. E.", "family": "Dahl", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"family": "Dong Yu", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"family": "Li Deng", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "A.", "family": "Acero", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["IEEE Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2012, 1]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[2012, 1]]}}, {"abstract": "<jats:p>Similarities and differences between speech and writing have been the subject of innumerable studies, but until now there has been no attempt to provide a unified linguistic analysis of the whole range of spoken and written registers in English. In this widely acclaimed empirical study, Douglas Biber uses computational techniques to analyse the linguistic characteristics of twenty three spoken and written genres, enabling identification of the basic, underlying dimensions of variation in English. In Variation Across Speech and Writing, six dimensions of variation are identified through a factor analysis, on the basis of linguistic co-occurence patterns. The resulting model of variation provides for the description of the distinctive linguistic characteristics of any spoken or written text andd emonstrates the ways in which the polarization of speech and writing has been misleading, and thus enables reconciliation of the contradictory conclusions reached in previous research.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1017/cbo9780511621024", "type": "monograph", "created": {"date-parts": [[2012, 6, 15]], "date-time": "2012-06-15T13:55:48Z", "timestamp": 1339768548000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 2032, "title": ["Variation across Speech and Writing"], "author": [{"given": "Douglas", "family": "Biber", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "issued": {"date-parts": [[1988, 10, 27]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[1988, 10, 27]]}}, {"DOI": "10.1016/j.neuroimage.2012.04.062", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2012, 5, 12]], "date-time": "2012-05-12T04:12:28Z", "timestamp": 1336795948000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 2007, "title": ["A review and synthesis of the first 20years of PET and fMRI studies of heard speech, spoken language and reading"], "author": [{"given": "Cathy J.", "family": "Price", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["NeuroImage"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2012, 8]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[2012, 8]]}}, {"abstract": "<jats:p>Oral speech intelligibility tests were conducted with, and without, supplementary visual observation of the speaker's facial and lip movements. The difference between these two conditions was examined as a function of the speech-to-noise ratio and of the size of the vocabulary under test. The visual contribution to oral speech intelligibility (relative to its possible contribution) is, to a first approximation, independent of the speech-to-noise ratio under test. However, since there is a much greater opportunity for the visual contribution at low speech-to-noise ratios, its absolute contribution can be exploited most profitably under these conditions.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1121/1.1907309", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2005, 9, 30]], "date-time": "2005-09-30T16:27:28Z", "timestamp": 1128097648000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 1967, "title": ["Visual Contribution to Speech Intelligibility in Noise"], "author": [{"given": "W. H.", "family": "Sumby", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "Human Factors Operations Research Laboratories, Washington 25, D. C."}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Irwin", "family": "Pollack", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Human Factors Operations Research Laboratories, Washington 25, D. C."}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "container-title": ["The Journal of the Acoustical Society of America"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[1954, 3, 1]]}, "published": {"date-parts": [[1954, 3, 1]]}}, {"DOI": "10.1109/tasl.2011.2114881", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2011, 2, 16]], "date-time": "2011-02-16T14:23:43Z", "timestamp": 1297866223000}, "is-referenced-by-count": 1951, "title": ["An Algorithm for Intelligibility Prediction of Time–Frequency Weighted Noisy Speech"], "author": [{"given": "Cees H.", "family": "Taal", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Richard C.", "family": "Hendriks", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Richard", "family": "Heusdens", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Jesper", "family": "Jensen", "sequence": "addit